LOGBOOK.md:10:- LIFECYCLE: One hundred real HEV start/traffic/stop cycles restored descriptors, sessions, queued batches, outstanding reads, boundary start/stop ownership, and HEV/bridge close stages; sampled physical footprint did not grow monotonically. Seven startup and seven cleanup cancellation stages plus 50 harness host-termination iterations passed.
LOGBOOK.md:11:- AVAILABILITY: `os_proc_available_memory` is explicitly unavailable on macOS in the public SDK. HEV queued bytes and process-wide Swift task counts also have no public gauge. All are recorded as unavailable/unknown; host memory, process threads, and proxy zeros were not substituted. Physical iPhone and sleep/wake remain named deferred gaps.
LOGBOOK.md:14:- REV2 AUTHENTICITY: Every final raw run is fail-closed on an exact candidate tree OID and reads the pinned HEV revision/source SHA-256 from `NativeDependencies/manifest.json`. Each emitting run performs and serializes its own 100-cycle descriptor/thread/session/read/queue/physical-footprint samples and derives monotonic growth from those values. The original signal 6 came from the retired `proc_pid_rusage` importer; the final sampler uses public `task_info(TASK_VM_INFO)` plus `proc_pidinfo(PROC_PIDTASKINFO)` and is exercised by three independent exact-tree runs.
LOGBOOK.md:17:- MEASUREMENT: The bounded loopback-only SPM harness ran 36 rows on a physical Apple M3 Max Mac (arm64, macOS 26.5): MTU 1500/4096/8500 × IPv4/native IPv6/dual stack × nominal/constrained-buffer/25ms receiver-stall/mixed bidirectional traffic. Nominal and mixed rows delivered 100%; pressure rows produced only named bounded effects and recovered with zero production-owned descriptor delta. Swift-task delta is explicitly unavailable because the synchronous runner owns no tasks; process threads are not treated as a proxy.
LOGBOOK.md:20:- GAPS: Physical iPhone remains `deferred-unavailable` under ADR-024. NAT64 is explicitly unavailable because no authorized deterministic local environment existed and route/Internet mutation was prohibited. Energy is unavailable from the unprivileged SPM harness; no sudo or `powermetrics` was used.
LOGBOOK.md:23:- REVISION 4: The writer now anchors every lexical directory component from the filesystem root during configuration parsing, retains the complete no-follow descriptor chain through write/rename, and revalidates device/inode identity before writing. A production-entry `RENAME_SWAP` regression covers an ancestor above `.temp`, alongside the root-symlink and output-parent attacks. A fresh 512-packet matrix and three 64-packet production repeats passed; the full suite passed 478 tests, and affected coverage is 86.08% regions, 90.35% functions, and 95.98% lines.
LOGBOOK.md:45:- SAFETY: Production signing, physical Gate P0, Developer ID archive, notarization, DMG publication, iOS, installation, app/provider launch, VPN preference save/activation, route mutation, and DNS mutation were not run. Documentation makes no shipping claim and routes physical work only through dedicated-host gate `TASK-260819-25e1ys`.
LOGBOOK.md:49:- ARCHITECTURE: The Release provider is universal `arm64`/`x86_64`, embeds `ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter` plus HEV/libssh2 production symbols, contains no `CReluxNativeFixture` or forbidden runtime-loader symbol, links only system frameworks/libraries, and embeds a checksum-valid four-platform relay payload. No signing overlay, embedded private-key payload, signing, installation, launch, VPN preference/tunnel, route, or DNS mutation was used.
LOGBOOK.md:61:- VERIFICATION: The focused negative suite, full credential-free matrix, Python/shell syntax checks, strict Swift format lint, and `git diff --check` exit 0. No signing, installation, launch, VPN preference/tunnel, route, or DNS operation was performed.
LOGBOOK.md:69:- IMPLEMENTATION: The generated workspace now has a minimal macOS host and one embedded packet-tunnel system extension at `Contents/Library/SystemExtensions/works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel.systemextension`. The host and provider use the r12 identifiers, macOS 15 floor, shared build/protocol version, target-owned plists, and separate Development/Developer ID entitlement files. The provider imports NetworkExtension and implements only empty start/stop plus version-message handling.
LOGBOOK.md:101:- VERDICT: Changes requested and routed to development; format lint, core-boundary validation, and diff checks remain clean.
LOGBOOK.md:140:- IMPLEMENTATION: The test now injects `ManualFixtureClock`, waits until the first queued open has registered its 300 ms timer, and advances exactly 300 ms to preserve the pre-admission timeout assertion. It then keeps the clock fixed while the second open queues and rekey resumes, removing wall-clock scheduling from the success boundary without extending a timeout, sleeping, skipping, or weakening the expected error.
LOGBOOK.md:167:- ROOT CAUSE: Peer EOF was published by `HEVUDPAssociationConnection.close` before `connectionClosed` removed the owner from the adapter and released queued-byte accounting. A peer-observable EOF therefore could not prove that adapter-internal snapshots had settled.
LOGBOOK.md:201:- DECISION: The 4096-byte bound is checked over the actual binary-property-list representation of the exact three-key `providerConfiguration`, so owner and manager-contract metadata count toward the NetworkExtension envelope. The macOS adapter reads only `NETunnelProviderProtocol.providerConfiguration`; it has no App Group, Keychain, route, network, packet, or profile-write path.
LOGBOOK.md:274:- REWORK: The race now signals cancellation before resuming its caller. The bounded ownership registry supports count-targeted retirement waiters, and a timed-out upload gives cooperative children at most 250 ms of the injected close budget to reconcile while permitting exactly one additional operation—the cancellation-ignoring source—to remain visible until release. The regression uses bounded monotonic state synchronization and fails instead of hanging if ownership cannot settle.
LOGBOOK.md:317:- REWORK: Socket-service cancellation now retires only its independently cancellable readiness operation. Channel opens may queue behind rekey under their original bounded deadline. One bounded shared rekey flight coalesces trigger reasons and lets a canceled caller detach while KEX continues. Per-channel FIFO gates retain exact write arguments across `EAGAIN`, with queued bytes still reserved against the caller cap. Returned credential algorithms must belong to both adapter capabilities and the request allowlist.
LOGBOOK.md:318:- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic loopback regressions prove canceled and timed-out reads leave sibling channels and the session ready; an open times out while rekey is deliberately suspended and a second queued open succeeds afterward; two rekey triggers produce one KEX while the first caller cancels; four concurrent same-channel payloads survive forced bridge backpressure byte-exactly; and a supported-but-disallowed credential algorithm fails before authentication.
LOGBOOK.md:323:- REWORK: A bounded FIFO session-operation gate now holds one permit across every channel-open, client-rekey, and keepalive/global-request `EAGAIN` sequence while independent channel payload I/O remains interleavable. Callback would-block paths return `-EAGAIN`. Timeout adaptation uses structured tasks and joins the losing branch; bridge shutdown marks terminal state, closes the injected connection, cancels and joins every queued service task, and only then discards buffers or frees engine state.
LOGBOOK.md:329:- REWORK: Socket progress now has one queued owner with exact-once partial drains. Channel reads enforce one pending operation per stream, writes reserve against the per-channel queued-byte cap, all pending gauges are live, and cancel/reset/close marks disposition then waits for tracked pointer users before bounded free. Session/open/connect teardown paths similarly drain active owners and serialize concurrent teardown.
LOGBOOK.md:492:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md:495:- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
LOGBOOK.md:500:- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
LOGBOOK.md:511:- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
LOGBOOK.md:513:- DECISION: Every NE value in the contract is now keyed by channel (`development` / `developer-id` / `app-store`), each profile row records `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues`, and the C1 scope is pinned to development-channel values so the ceremony cannot be pointed at the wrong one. Consequence worth remembering: the probe family is development-signed only, so it can prove the **development** entitlement shape and nothing else — r1 implied it proved the production shape.
LOGBOOK.md:522:- FINDING: Apple completed Developer-website support for iOS-style app group IDs on macOS on **21 Feb 2025**, and Xcode 16.3+ makes it the macOS default (forums/thread/721701). This project is new on Xcode 26.5, so both platforms now claim the identical registered literal.
LOGBOOK.md:552:- FINDING: A generation ledger consulted *inside* the actor cannot recover call order that was already lost at the task-spawn boundary. Ordering has to be captured where the caller still exists — on the caller's thread — not where the work is executed.
LOGBOOK.md:589:### 0421 — Security-testing review now routes to the provider that previously filter-failed on it (TASK-260728-1glezz)
LOGBOOK.md:590:- FINDING: The new serial policy makes review Codex-owned and marks security, networking, DNS, parsers, signing, and release tasks `review=required`. The superseded `docs/spawn-policy.md` section it replaced recorded that Codex `gpt-5.6-sol` trips OpenAI's cybersecurity content filter on legitimate defensive security-testing work (fuzzing, hostile-input corpora, DNS/route leak tests, redaction tests) and exits non-zero mid-run.
LOGBOOK.md:591:- ANOMALY: Removing that section was required by the task AC, so the highest-risk review categories are now routed to the provider with the known failure mode. Whether the filter still reproduces on `gpt-5.6-sol` is unverified — no security-testing review has run under the new policy yet.
LOGBOOK.md:598:- SCOPE: `task-board.config.json`, `docs/spawn-policy.md`, `README.md:90`, `.spec/goal-macos-v1.md:35`, `.gitignore`. Commit policy (20:00–09:00 MSK window, `+04:00` timestamps, strictly monotonic ≥1min in accepted-task order, push, local-origin equality) lives in `version_control.desired_commit_time` and is mirrored as a numbered procedure in the doc. Git identity and SSH signing untouched — no local overrides introduced.
LOGBOOK.md:611:- VERDICT: Changes requested and routed to `analysis`. Use a true JCS implementation or close the jq-equivalent domain over safe integers and negative-zero/number-form constraints, add this expansion as a negative regression, and propagate the corrected test/copies. All retained schema, m=3, authority/status, latency, diagram, copy, board, privacy, and 332-test gates pass.
LOGBOOK.md:628:- VERDICT: Changes requested and routed to `analysis`. Original hostile fixture, DNS equality/one-over, canonical hashes, warning-free diagram, 57 task references/50 titles, byte-identical copies, board/diff/privacy checks, and all 332 Swift tests otherwise pass. Evidence: `TASK-260715-2kchi0_re-review-01-verdict-20260722.md`.
LOGBOOK.md:639:- DECISION: Protocol v1 fixes synthetic workload generation, ten deterministic seeds, controlled impairment profiles, warmup/steady/cooldown schedules, sampling, paired bootstrap and practical-effect rules, non-tradeable byte/DNS/route/resource/memory/failure/drop/cleanup gates, content-addressed raw references, capture authorization, privacy classes, and immutable pass/red/unavailable/invalid outcomes before tuning begins. macOS named-device rows are current; iPhone slots are schema-compatible deferred rows and are not guessed from Mac evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:651:- FINDING: The seed threat model stated several accepted designs as shipped mitigations even though the repository has no concrete packet-tunnel provider target, selected production SSH adapter, Keychain vault, DNS/route composition, relay deployment/frame-pump composition, degraded-mode controller, platform fail-closed implementation, Sparkle path, or application release pipeline.
LOGBOOK.md:654:- RESIDUAL: Production SSH selection (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), DNS runtime authorization (`TASK-260721-3miqh4`), physical route/DNS evidence, credentials/entitlements, relay composition, update/release controls, and independent candidate review remain explicit evidence gates.
LOGBOOK.md:658:- SECURITY: Manifest, identity, and executable reads use one regular-file `O_NOFOLLOW` descriptor; executable size and SHA-256 come from the same open descriptor, removing the prior path-swap window. `test-relay-shell-artifacts.sh` captures exact stdout/stderr in a mode-0700/umask-077 temporary directory and invokes the comparator before stdio smoke for native Darwin arm64 and Rosetta amd64.
LOGBOOK.md:680:- FINDING: The typed `queueSaturated` / `datagramTooLarge` path preserves the HEV descriptor and association ID, but `HEVUDPDatagramAdapter.observeNonterminalRelayError` validates ownership through `ClientUDPAssociationRegistry.resolveRemoteDatagram`. That API is explicitly datagram activity: it cancels the existing timer, advances the idle deadline, schedules a replacement timer, and increments `activityUpdates`. A relay error-only pressure observation therefore prolongs association lifetime, contrary to the rework contract that nonterminal error accounting be an aggregate observation only.
LOGBOOK.md:686:- LIFECYCLE: Deterministic fake-relay regressions prove both nonterminal signals permit later outbound and inbound datagrams on the exact same association ID, followed by an independent peer close/ack. Finite and unknown terminal errors in error-first and close-first orderings produce one descriptor close, one registry release, one relay close, and zero retained queue bytes per owner.
LOGBOOK.md:697:- ADDRESS: Resolver results with a non-empty IPv6 zone are discarded before unmapping, accepted-result byte credit, family/socket admission, or send. An all-scoped result set closes its original bounded reservation with zero descriptors/sends and zero retained scheduler/registry resources; a later in-cap unzoned IPv6 result is selected byte-exactly without zone rewriting. The system socket seam also rejects zoned IPv6 defensively.
LOGBOOK.md:706:- REWORK: Domain sends now reserve socketless association capacity and an exact generation/incarnation token before resolver admission. A fixed worker pool and fixed queued-job/completion credit bound copied names, payloads, inspected/accepted results, retained result bytes, deadlines, and concurrent lookups; `Send` returns `pendingResolution` without blocking the event-loop caller. Completion attaches a family socket only through the original token.
LOGBOOK.md:707:- LIFECYCLE: Registry retirement cancels an incarnation-local context on close, idle expiry, generation replacement, session loss, process shutdown, or parent cancellation. Active and queued jobs recheck that context before resolver/socket work, and token-scoped socket admission prevents a paused old lookup from reopening or sending through a same-generation reused ID. Deterministic barriers restore job, queue, name, payload, result, completion, worker, association, timer, socket, and descriptor counts to their declared baselines.
LOGBOOK.md:708:- ADDRESS/PRECEDENCE: IPv4-mapped IPv6 is rejected before association or descriptor admission. `MSG_TRUNC` now returns before sockaddr conversion/materialization, so a truncated reply with an otherwise unsupported/scoped source remains a counted nonterminal oversize drop. Numeric/domain and reply-source byte preservation, readiness-only retry, round-robin budgets, and privacy-safe diagnostics remain unchanged.
LOGBOOK.md:720:- RESOLVER/FAIRNESS: Domain use validates the accepted ASCII presentation form (including numeric-text and malformed A-label rejection) before a cancellable resolver seam. Concurrent requests, deadline, inspected result count, copied result bytes, family selection, turn targets, socket visits, datagrams, bytes, and monotonic time all have immutable checked caps. The inspected-result cap is independent of accepted results, so invalid resolver output cannot force an unbounded scan. Names, addresses, payloads, and raw resolver/socket errors never enter diagnostics or metric dimensions.
LOGBOOK.md:725:- REWORK: `EnsureFamilies` now reserves the complete canonical IPv4/IPv6 family set before opening either descriptor. A failure closes every staged descriptor and any socket already owned by that association exactly once, then restores association, socket, logical timer, event, and physical timer state to baseline. Lazy single-family admission remains available, but a failed later family completion also retires the partial association.
LOGBOOK.md:726:- RACE EVIDENCE: A private owner-loop seam submits an explicit obsolete timer arm after rearm and proves the arm counter increments without changing the live association or its one current logical/physical timer. Barrier-controlled tests repeat descriptor-use and activity races against local close, remote close, expiry, generation replacement, session close, session loss, process termination, and parent cancellation; each path proves its permitted linearization, stale token rejection or terminal owner closure, exactly-once descriptor close, and baseline restoration.
LOGBOOK.md:729:### 1527 — Relay UDP registry owns bounded unbound descriptors (TASK-260715-xw5dxc)
LOGBOOK.md:730:- IMPLEMENTATION: A single Go owner goroutine now linearizes relay association admission, descriptor use, activity, close, expiry, generation replacement, and cancellation. Identity is the session generation plus nonzero client association ID plus a monotonically increasing local incarnation, so stale timer/activity/close work cannot affect a reused numeric ID.
LOGBOOK.md:731:- RESOURCE POLICY: Association, family-specific socket, logical timer, and pending-close credits are checked before descriptor creation and report finite aggregate counters. One indexed deadline heap and one physical monotonic timer bound timer/task state; expiry events consume reserved pending-close credit until observed. IPv4 and IPv6 descriptors are created lazily and closed exactly once across normal, partial-failure, expiry, session, process, and cancellation paths.
LOGBOOK.md:732:- SOCKET POLICY: Production descriptors are standard-library UDP sockets configured nonblocking and close-on-exec. They are unconnected and deliberately unbound at registry creation; no public listener or privileged port is created. A later bounded `sendto` owner may allow the kernel to assign an ephemeral source port without changing registry ownership.
LOGBOOK.md:733:- VERIFICATION: Pinned Go 1.26.5 focused tests passed 20 repeated runs and the race detector; targeted lifecycle tests passed 100 repeated runs; full relay tests, vet, and build passed. `make relay-protocol-check` passed schema/vector/drift checks and 57 protocol tests; the full Swift suite passed 318 tests and Swift build passed. Real descriptor tests prove IPv4/IPv6 family, `O_NONBLOCK`, `FD_CLOEXEC`, port zero, unconnected state, and post-close `EBADF`; board, formatting, diff, privacy, and resource scans are clean.
LOGBOOK.md:752:- GENERATION: Relay timers, attempts, sessions, health/process/channel watchers, and association callbacks now require a non-null M2 relay-work registration with a monotonic work generation, attempt generation when applicable, and finite phase. Retirement clears that registration before cleanup, publication, or rescheduling, so a late callback from attempt N is stale even before N+1 exists.
LOGBOOK.md:753:- RETRY/REASON: T15 is the sole same-runtime re-entry after exhaustion or a local-change-required relay result. It accepts only a higher locally authorized relay-only policy generation on the current healthy base, allocates a fresh bounded recovery cycle, and cannot accept remote/UI input or overlap M3 path/host/route/lane/sleep/NAT64/captive ownership. Successful startup activation publishes local finite `activation_ready` with all capabilities false until atomic full publication changes the reason to `none`.
LOGBOOK.md:758:- REVIEW: Changes requested and routed to architecture analysis. The declared current-event tuple leaves a retired relay attempt numerically current until the next attempt is allocated, so late health/process/channel/timer callbacks need an explicit current registration or retirement token before the contract is generation-safe.
LOGBOOK.md:764:- OWNERSHIP: M2 exclusively owns relay bootstrap/session loss and one bounded relay-only reprobe while the current base stays healthy; it remains publicly connected/degraded and never sets provider reasserting. M3 exclusively owns path, host, route, mandatory/lane-pool, sleep/wake, NAT64, and captive reconnect. A current M3 event cancels M2 timers/attempts before any M3 retry loop begins.
LOGBOOK.md:786:- RELIABILITY/CLEANUP: Event traces now cover both IPv4→IPv6 and IPv6→IPv4 orders, cancellation/tombstone/late-response races, duplicate attempts before suppression, every M2 fallback trigger, malformed/mismatched no-shopping, same-endpoint TCP, later promotion, and exact attempt/terminal counts. Transactions, queue bytes, reservations, tombstones, connections, connection buffers, fixed bytes, and descriptors return to observed zero after every regenerated run.
LOGBOOK.md:790:- EVIDENCE/DECISION PROPOSED: Default policy is 4 endpoints, exact 65,535-byte DNS messages, 16 live owners, 256 KiB queued wire, 4 MiB aggregate DNS, and 2/5/30/8/10-second open/response/logical/startup/idle values; hard envelope is 8 endpoints, 32 owners, 1 MiB queue, 8 MiB aggregate, and 5/10/120/40/30 seconds. The full-wire message value is fixed rather than tunable so memory policy cannot truncate legitimate DNS/TCP responses.
LOGBOOK.md:800:- SAFETY/PLANNING: Resolver/policy validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> authenticated SSH/relay -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration. TASK-260721-33o8fc owns schema/codec/migration, TASK-260721-2raag7 owns M4 profile experience, and revised TASK-260715-5o6jqg/TASK-260715-28jdml/TASK-260715-336ljl own shared-failure, M2, and integration evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:804:- DECISION: `TCPAdmissionRegistry` is one generation-scoped mutex authority for pending handshakes, authenticated flows, concurrent channel opens, owned channels, fixed directional queue capacity, live buffered bytes, and phase counts. All ceilings and the measured-safe/HEV evidence gates are caller-injected; denial is synchronous, retains no waiter, maps to SOCKS5 `REP=01`, and explicitly prohibits an SSH open. Reservation identifiers are monotonically allocated without reuse and fail closed with finite `identifier_capacity` pressure before `UInt64` wrap.
LOGBOOK.md:807:- VERIFICATION: Eighteen admission tests cover below/at/above every independent ceiling, 128/256-way reserve races, 500 release/open and 500 late-callback races, 250 explicit-release/deinit ownership races, ID exhaustion, queued-byte overflow, session-health races, 8,000 sustained pressure rejects alongside 2,000 admitted-flow progress steps, every terminal reason across 320 churn lifecycles, and exact local/exported baseline recovery. The bounded-queue convergence probe passes 20/20 runs; focused normal and TSan admission/diagnostics suites pass; `make validate-core` passes boundary/native gates, 306 tests in 27 suites, and the post-test build; strict recursive Swift format, privacy/boundary, diff, and board validation pass.
LOGBOOK.md:816:- CORRECTION: The normative ownership sequence now matches `HEVSOCKSBoundary.swift:284-299`: immediately after TCP accept/socket setup, an already-stopped or generation-retired boundary or a full pending-authentication set closes the descriptor without a SOCKS5 method reply, RFC 1929 reply, authentication enqueue, or adapter handoff.
LOGBOOK.md:817:- BOUNDARY: Wrong/stale capability and the M1 monotonic accept-to-auth deadline remain authentication-phase failures with only phase-and-remaining-budget-safe replies. The post-handoff adapter resource reservation is separate from the boundary pending-authentication ceiling.
LOGBOOK.md:821:- DECISION: The shared iOS/macOS HEV boundary remains IPv4 loopback plus a fresh per-generation RFC 1929 capability; loopback/TCP peer metadata is explicitly not process identity. A descriptor reaches `SSHProxyAdapter` only after capability proof and is positioned at the first CONNECT-request byte.
LOGBOOK.md:822:- CORRECTION: Current `HEVSOCKSBoundary` uses `SO_RCVTIMEO`, which is a per-receive inactivity bound and can restart as bytes arrive; authentication sends have no deadline. The pending set is count-bounded, but current source does not prove an absolute accept-to-auth deadline. M1 must compute one monotonic deadline at accept, apply its remaining budget across greeting/auth reads, comparisons, and replies without progress extension, and prove slow-trickle/reply-stall eviction plus slot/descriptor release on iOS and macOS.
LOGBOOK.md:836:- REWORK: Normalize thrown NetworkExtension start errors at the thin adapter boundary (or through an equivalently shared injectable translator), preserve unknown domain/code, cover both iOS/macOS seams plus the disabled-manager production path, and rerun focused normal/TSan, full core, formatting/diff/board checks, and both adapter builds before another reviewer cycle.
LOGBOOK.md:846:- REGRESSION: Both NetworkExtension adapters read `NEVPNConnection.status` before installing `NEVPNStatusDidChange`, so a terminal transition in that gap could be lost and an already-stopped session could falsely reach the 15-second `stopTimedOut` path.
LOGBOOK.md:852:- REGRESSION: Actor isolation did not serialize repository calls across suspended NetworkExtension callbacks, so concurrent operations could load and mutate overlapping manager generations. Both platform seams also used `NSNumber.intValue`, truncating `1.5` to current version 1 and overflowing `UInt64.max` into repairable corruption.
LOGBOOK.md:864:- DECISION: `ReluxTunnelCore` owns the serialized exact-predicate repository, immutable canonical configuration, 15-second callback tokens, stable errors, one-stale retry, and reload verification. The iOS/macOS adapter modules only translate public `NetworkExtension` objects and notifications into the injectable core seams.
LOGBOOK.md:874:- COVERAGE: Shared tests run through iOS and macOS seams for all four commands, hostile/oversized/future/mutating inputs, duplicates, nil handlers, retirement, all raw Apple reasons, startup and cleanup deadlines, stop-wins-start, fatal handoff, deallocation, and two 100-cycle baselines. Existing coordinator tests retain every startup acquisition/cancellation point and route-clear uncertainty coverage.
LOGBOOK.md:903:- SAFETY: Settings are requested only after validated configuration, authenticated SSH, TCP readiness, SSH-only safe DNS readiness, pure packet-plane preparation, and mandatory health checks. Unknown apply failures are conservatively treated as an uncertain commit and cleared; definite non-commit skips clear. A clear failure publishes TCP/DNS false while truthfully retaining `routesInstalled=true` and `routeState=clearFailed`.
LOGBOOK.md:915:- DECISION: `RuntimeDiagnosticsStore` owns one bounded generation with 96 counters, 56 gauges, one cumulative DNS-latency histogram, and at most one cataloged error per each of 10 finite domains. Packet/HEV use its `TunnelMetrics` recorder, SSH uses the same recorder without lane identity, and coordinator/TCP/DNS/route/memory use typed finite-category updates; unknown free-form metric names are counted then discarded without retaining the label.
LOGBOOK.md:917:- PRIVACY: The default schema has no lane/run/flow IDs, DNS or destination fields, addresses, credentials, commands/stdin, payload content, or traffic samples. State transition times are monotonic process-uptime milliseconds; errors retain only validated domain/code; flow, memory, route, health, packet/byte, drop-reason, DNS-result, and SSH values are aggregates.
LOGBOOK.md:924:- SAFETY: A future lifecycle `routeState` previously decoded as `unknown` while preserving true TCP, safe-DNS, route-installed, and health facts. Route state now participates in the shared fail-safe projection for both decoded and directly constructed lifecycle snapshots, forcing every capability false when it is unknown.
LOGBOOK.md:931:- CAPABILITY: TCP, safe DNS, UDP, route mode, route-installed state, and health are independent required facts with no full-mode bit. Unknown output lifecycle/route values project to `unknown` with every capability false; generation/sequence ordering rejects lower generations and non-increasing snapshots.
LOGBOOK.md:938:- SAFETY: Settings clear is called only after a committed or uncertain apply. A required failed/uncertain clear publishes capabilities false but truthfully retains routesInstalled=true with routeState=clearFailed; a clear that was unnecessary or confirmed publishes routesInstalled=false. Legacy version protocol v1 remains byte-exact while new M1 commands/snapshots use explicit schemas.
LOGBOOK.md:939:- REVIEW: Independent agent review requested and verified corrections across ownership, source-interface feasibility, versions, dependencies, transitions, and route cleanup, then accepted all five AC. PlantUML is unavailable and local Graphviz is broken by missing libltdl; authoritative sources and the toolchain anomaly are attached rather than claiming rendered evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:1047:- MILESTONE: BUG-260720-2p4fln accepted → done; TASK-260715-35wctc unblocked. Product fixes (pre-fini stats snapshot, coalesced single boundary stop, lwIP test-lifecycle discipline) and rework-01 descriptor-gate isolation all verified.
LOGBOOK.md:1048:- VERIFICATION: Second reviewer confirmed rework design: both former `openDescriptorCount() == baseline` gates (`Tests/ReluxTunnelNativeAdapterTests/HEVBridgeIntegrationTests.swift:230,275`) now assert harness-owned release only — boundary starts/stops 1/1, live SOCKS channels 0, queued batches/outstanding reads 0, both endpoint-close lifecycle stages reached, `packet_bridge_cleanup_close_error_total` 0 — with global baseline/observed/delta demoted to failure diagnostics. Isolation-safe under parallel suites, leak-detection intent preserved.
LOGBOOK.md:1049:- VERIFICATION: Reviewer reran on the exact tree: `swift build` clean, full `swift test` 110/110 (HEV suite 13/13 incl. 100-cycle), `swift test --sanitize=thread --filter HEVIntegrationTests` 13/13 with zero TSan reports (grep-verified), `swift format lint --strict` clean, `make check-core-boundaries check-native-dependencies` clean. Repeatability per prior evidence: implementer 30× full runs HEV 30/30, orchestrator 20× full runs 0 HEV failures.
LOGBOOK.md:1052:### 1402 — HEV descriptor leak checks now isolate harness-owned resources (BUG-260720-2p4fln rework)
LOGBOOK.md:1053:- FIX: The fault and 100-cycle real-HEV regressions no longer use `openDescriptorCount() == baseline` as a pass/fail condition. Each harness instead records its own boundary stop, live SOCKS channels, packet-flow reads/queues, bridge endpoint-close lifecycle stages, and cleanup-close errors; the process-global descriptor baseline/observed delta is retained only in failure diagnostics.
LOGBOOK.md:1061:### 1349 — HEV 100-cycle descriptor-baseline check races cross-suite parallelism (review BUG-260720-2p4fln)
LOGBOOK.md:1063:- ROOT CAUSE: `openDescriptorCount()` counts process-global descriptors, but Swift Testing runs suites in parallel; flake log `.temp/BUG-260720-2p4fln/review/hev-flake-85.log` shows the fuzz suite still live when the 100-cycle test captured its baseline. Any concurrent suite's descriptor delta between baseline capture and final check breaks exact equality permanently; the 5s poll then times out. `.serialized` only guards within the HEV suite. Same fragile pattern at `HEVBridgeIntegrationTests.swift:230` (fault test, 3s).
LOGBOOK.md:1064:- DECISION: Review verdict — product changes accepted on evidence (stats snapshot, single boundary stop, quit-safety, TSan, lint, boundaries all green); task routed to-dev only to make the two descriptor-baseline assertions isolation-safe under full-package parallel runs and re-demonstrate repeatability.
LOGBOOK.md:1070:- EXPECTATION FIX: A stalled SOCKS peer does not guarantee Darwin endpoint-B backpressure because native HEV may continue draining the tunnel descriptor. The stall test now asserts the deterministic contract (`sent + would-block drops + ENOBUFS drops == received`) and allows the legitimate zero-drop schedule instead of forcing timing-dependent loss.
LOGBOOK.md:1080:- MILESTONE: Deterministic seeded fuzz suite (`Tests/ReluxTunnelCoreTests/PacketFrameFuzzTests.swift`, written by prior codex run, verified unchanged) proves the p89bdj bounds contract on both framing directions: 50k hostile frames/direction pass with reverse peak malloc growth 12.7 MiB (256 MiB ceiling), forward 407 KiB, ~3.9 s/direction, monotonic drop counters, all four malformed reasons exercised, privacy-safe log keys. CI-scale run (512/direction) inside plain `swift test`: 105/105 pass; TSan run clean.
LOGBOOK.md:1100:### 1200 — Bounded libssh2 observation fork clears the remaining public-API clauses (TASK-260720-2sltje)
LOGBOOK.md:1101:- DECISION: The existing libssh2 patch remains a single allowlisted six-file delta. It adds typed server-initiated KEX `STARTED`/`SUCCEEDED`/`FAILED` observation with a monotonic generation and one bounded, nonblocking reply-correlated global-request operation; packet ordering, encryption, MAC, KEX algorithms, and crypto implementations remain unchanged.
LOGBOOK.md:1156:- IMPACT: Every positive overlay inherits the mismatched base hashes, while the downstream `TASK-260721-2ohf99` semantic rules explicitly require rejection of hash-mismatched rows. Rework-02 is routed to `analysis`; all other schema, m=3, authority, status, latency, copy, privacy, diagram, board, and 332-test gates pass.
LOGBOOK.md:1166:- IMPLEMENTATION: Child initial window/cap/adjustment policy is set through a normal pre-open channel option and observed through snapshots/events; returned credit is earned only after pipeline delivery. Client manual, per-direction protected-byte, and injected-monotonic-time rekeys share the existing KEX state machine, coalesce concurrent reasons/promises, reset generation counters on success, and observe server-initiated KEX without product reflection or a test-only API.
LOGBOOK.md:1187:- ROOT CAUSE: Any ordering where HEV main returns before `requestStop()` — init failure, or the contract-modeled "unexpected HEV main return while active" — makes `supervisedCleanup` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/PacketFlowBridge.swift:1017`) invoke quit after fini: infinite 100 ms `usleep` loop on a cooperative executor thread; provider `stop()` never returns, descriptors never close. Plain `stop()` racing a just-died HEV hits the same path.
LOGBOOK.md:1195:- DECISION: The real `DescriptorBorrowConsumer` runs pinned HEV `ad760049` on exactly one dedicated pthread, calls the public quit API once, joins before return, and never closes or duplicates bridge endpoint B. A process-wide lease protects HEV's global C state.
LOGBOOK.md:1197:- VERIFICATION: The emitted in-memory YAML records caller MTU, `udp: tcp`, task stack 24576, TCP buffer 4096, `udp-copy-buffer-nums: 2`, and max sessions 1200. The generator rejects any stack input the pinned parser would silently raise.
LOGBOOK.md:1203:- VERIFICATION: A native probe linked directly against the checksum-verified rebuilt XCFramework reports `task-stack-size=35480`, `tcp-buffer-size=4096`, `udp-copy-buffer-nums=10`, and `max-session-count=1200`; evidence is `.temp/TASK-260715-1vv52g/hev-effective-config-probe-01.log`.
LOGBOOK.md:1232:- VERIFICATION: Reviewer independently reran swift build, swift test (39/39 in 5 suites), swift test --sanitize=thread --filter PacketFlowBridge (13/13, zero TSan reports), swift format lint --strict, make check-core-boundaries, and prohibition greps. Evidence: .task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-3o0co4/TASK-260715-3o0co4_review.md.
